      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218283/assets/2f877bbec2e880c8a64979997cb8694390f568ee7d2ad240d3a38f07de04157d.png)\n\nThe graph of $g(x)=f(x)+2$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagram, the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ passes through the point $(0,-2).$\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by translating $2$ units *up*. \n\nSo, the graph $y=f(x)$ would be $2$ units *below* the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218283/assets/34927fd5b2a6245822c4d39ad38e15449d18b068b57dc6f7d7468037b1e53a59.png)",
